.TH FLEET 1 "October 2026" "cockpit-fleet" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fleet \- drive Ansible through cockpit-fleet from a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fleet
.I area verb
.RB [ \-\-param
.IR value ]...
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B fleet launch
.I template
.RB [ \-a
.IR name = value ]...
.RB [ \-\-limit
.IR pattern ]
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RB [ \-\-diff ]
.RB [ \-\-serial ]
.RB [ \-\-detach ]
.br
.B fleet log
.I run
.RB [ \-f ]
.br
.B fleet result
.I run
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.br
.B fleet secret
.I credential field
.br
.B fleet secret \-\-channel
.I channel
.br
.B fleet help
.RI [ area " [" verb ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fleet
does from a terminal what the Fleet page of Cockpit does, with the role the
user has there: it is for the members of the groups
.BR cockpit\-fleet\-viewer ,
.B cockpit\-fleet\-operator
and
.BR cockpit\-fleet\-admin ,
and for those of
.BR cockpit\-fleet\-approver ,
who approve the runs of the job templates that need it
.RB ( "fleet approval list" ,
.BR "fleet approval approve \-\-id" " RUN)."
A run launched with
.B fleet launch
from such a template waits for that, and is followed once it starts.
It has no privilege of its own: every command is a call to the backend
through
.BR sudo (8),
checked as one made from the interface and, when it changes something,
recorded in the audit trail
with
.B cli
in its "via" column.
.PP
Every command of the backend is
.B fleet
followed by its area and verb, such as
.B fleet run list
or
.BR "fleet template list" ;
.B fleet help
lists those of the caller's role and
.B fleet help
.I area verb
says what one takes. Parameters are options:
.B \-\-status failed
or
.BR \-\-status=failed ,
a flag alone for true and
.B \-\-no\-
before it for false, a list by repeating the option, JSON for an object.
A text or JSON value is read from a file with
.BI @ path\fR,
from the standard input with
.BR @\- ;
.B @@
is a plain @. A command that takes an id also takes it alone, and the id of
a template, project, inventory, credential, check, host, schedule, channel or
rule may be given by its name.
.B project upload
takes the archive from a file with
.BI \-\-archive " path"\fR,
or from the standard input with
.BR "\-\-archive \-" .
.B store put
takes the file to upload with
.BI \-\-file " path"\fR,
and goes on from what an earlier upload of the same file to the same
.B \-\-path
left with
.BR \-\-resume .
.B store read
writes the file of the store named by
.B \-\-path
to
.BI \-\-output " path"\fR,
by default to its name in the current directory, or to the standard output
with
.BR "\-\-output \-" .
The completion for bash offers commands and options, the choices of an
option, and the names of what the backend lists for the caller's role.
.SH PROJECTS AT A TERMINAL
An administrator works on projects at the terminal in two ways.
.PP
A project written in the interface is cloned with
.BI "fleet project clone " "project " \fR[\fIdirectory\fR],
changed and committed there with git, and sent back with
.BR "fleet project push" ,
which the backend takes only on top of the revision the project still has,
as a line of commits whose files the editor could have written; its
playbooks are then checked as when saved in the interface.
.B fleet project pull
brings into the clone what was written in the interface meanwhile.
An inventory written in the interface, a tree of files, is cloned, pulled
and pushed the same way:
.BI "fleet inventory clone " "inventory " \fR[\fIdirectory\fR],
.BR "fleet inventory pull " and
.BR "fleet inventory push" ;
a push reads the inventory again.
.PP
A project may also be a directory under
.B [projects] local_roots
of config.toml, written by the administrators of Fleet and read by the
service. The convention, which the self-check verifies (setfacl is in the
package acl):
.PP
.nf
sudo install \-d \-m 2770 \-g cockpit\-fleet\-admin /srv/ansible
sudo setfacl \-m u:cockpit\-fleet:rX \-m d:u:cockpit\-fleet:rX /srv/ansible
# in /etc/cockpit\-fleet/config.toml: [projects] local_roots = ["/srv/ansible"]
.fi
.PP
then, with a umask that lets the group read (022 or 002):
.PP
.nf
git clone https://git.example.org/site.git /srv/ansible/site
fleet project create \-\-name site \-\-kind local \-\-path /srv/ansible/site
.fi
.PP
A root, or a project, under /home, /root, /run/user, /tmp or /var/tmp is
refused: the scheduler does not see them. To keep a project in a home, where
an editor or a synchronised folder has it, bind-mount it under the root,
and give the service account read access to the project itself:
.PP
.nf
sudo install \-d /srv/ansible/site
# in /etc/fstab:
/home/anna/site /srv/ansible/site none bind,x\-systemd.requires\-mounts\-for=/home 0 0
sudo systemctl daemon\-reload && sudo mount /srv/ansible/site
.fi
.SH SHORTCUTS
.TP
.B launch
Launches a job template, by name or id, follows its output and says how it
went. The answers to its survey are given with
.BR \-a ;
a required one left out is asked for at the terminal, and so is every secret
question, which is never taken from the command line: the value goes to the
backend on the standard input of
.BR fleet\-secret .
With
.BR \-\-serial ,
the run waits in the queue while another run is running on its inventory or
one launched before it is queued there, whatever their outcome.
.TP
.B log
Prints the output of a run;
.B \-f
follows it while the run goes on, then says how it went.
.TP
.B result
How a run went, per host and task by task;
.B \-\-wait
waits for its end.
.TP
.B secret
Sets a secret of a credential, or with
.B \-\-channel
the secret of a notification channel (administrators only), asked for twice at
the terminal or read from the standard input. It cannot be read back.
.TP
.B assistant
Talks to the agent of the chat in this terminal, its only tools those of
fleet\-mcp with the caller's role under the ceiling set for agents; with more
than one agent, its name chooses. It needs an agent with a program for a
terminal in config.toml.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Prints the answer as the backend gives it, for a program.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the command succeeded and, for a run that was followed, when the run
succeeded; 1 when that run failed, was canceled or ended in error; 2 for a
mistake in the command line; 3 when the backend refused the command or could
not be reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
fleet run list \-\-status failed
fleet launch "Say hello" \-a greeting=ciao \-\-limit web01 \-\-check
fleet log \-f r3c9d2e1f0a4b
fleet check run disk \-\-host\-id web01
fleet project upload shipped \-\-archive site.tar.gz
fleet store put \-\-file Win11.iso \-\-path win_iso/Win11.iso
fleet store fetch \-\-url https://example.org/setup.msi \-\-path apps/setup.msi
fleet file write p1a2b3c4d5e6 \-\-path site.yml \-\-content @site.yml \e
    \-\-expected SHA256_OF_THE_VERSION_READ
printf '%s' "$TOKEN" | fleet secret git\-token token
fleet secret \-\-channel telegram
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudo (8),
.BR ansible\-playbook (1)
